cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific access and
  usability views K_i, and actor-specific expectation-forming states P_i
  condition actions and decisions A_i. K_i is not required to be a set-theoretic
  subset of K; it represents actor-relative access, usability, exercisability,
  or referenceability with respect to the shared resource world. Delta K(S, tau)
  denotes realized real-resource change under an observation and accounting
  specification S and interval tau, where S includes resource coordinates,
  accounting boundary, and transformation convention. The same Delta K type can
  be used across micro and macro scales by changing S, tau, and aggregation rules.
  E_i[Delta K(S, tau)] denotes an actor-specific expected resource change where
  the relevant Delta K is represented on a quantitative projection for which an
  expectation is defined. Preferences, desirability, credit assessments, beliefs,
  and related structures remain in P_i rather than being identified with the
  expectation itself. VFT does not fix a universal probability measure,
  expectation-formation equation, action-selection rule, market-equilibrium
  condition, or aggregation law. Economic equilibrium, budget constraints,
  accounting identities, surplus, decentralized adjustment, micro-macro linkage,
  and reputation- or credit-economy models are treated as domain-specific
  projection candidates requiring additional economic and measurement assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - value-field theory
  - multidimensional resource state
  - shared resource world
  - actor-specific access
  - expectation stock
  - expected interval change
  - micro-macro linkage
  - reputation economy
  - credit economy
